            "target_entity_type": "Project",
            "paragraph": "To project potential future climate change scenarios in this study, the general circulation models (GCMs) under the Shared Socio-economic Pathways (SSPs) scenarios, initiated by the Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change's (IPCC) Coupled Model Intercomparison Project Phase 6 (CMIP6) database, were selected [17, 19]. Among the five SSPs of CMIP6, the SSP126 (minimum GHG emissions scenario) represents a sustainable green development path that belongs to a low GHG emissions climate scenario, limiting global warming to <2 \u00b0C by 2100; SSP245 (medium GHG emissions scenario) corresponds to a medium GHG emissions climate scenario, indicating that global temperatures will rise about 3-3.5 \u00b0C in the future; SSP585 (maximum GHG emissions scenario) denotes a high population growth and high fossil fuel consumption climate scenario, ranking as the worst climate scenario for GHG emissions and setting the upper limit of temperature rise to 4.7\u20135.1 \u00b0C [20, 34, 35]. The three SSPs were selected for two periods: the 2050s (2040-2060) and the 2070s (2060-2080), for a more effective and complete projection of species habitat distributions under twenty-first century climate change. The SSP370 was not considered in this study to avoid redundancy with SSP585, as both represent high emissions pathways. The HadGEM3-GC31-LL climate model, developed by the Met Office Hadley Centre [36], was chosen for projections in this study due to its high simulation capability for Bangladesh, as noted in previous studies such as Mohammed et al. [37] and Rifath et al. [38].",
